  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: A dry weather pattern will dominate\nthrough Friday as an upper ridge builds over the region. The \nbuilding ridge will allow for a gradual warming trend with high \ntemperatures peaking on Friday in the mid 60s to mid 70s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: The ridge flattens on Saturday as a quick\nmoving cold front sweeps across the region. A combination of\nswift westerly flow, and the bulk of the moisture with this\nfront in the low to mid levels of the atmosphere, will keep\nprecipitation focused near the Cascade crest with dry weather\nfor Central and Eastern Washington into the Idaho Panhandle. The\ncold front will result in breezy to locally windy conditions\nacross the region especially the typical spots such as the East\nSlopes of the Cascades, Columbia Basin, West Plains, and\nPalouse. The NBM is carrying a 60-90 percent chance of wind\ngusts exceeding 30 MPH in these areas, with a 50-60 percent\nchance of gusts exceeding 40 MPH on the Waterville Plateau.\nGiven the dry soils in the Columbia Basin can not rule out\npatchy blowing dust with the winds on Saturday. On Sunday as\nthis front exits dry weather and light winds return. Cooler and\ndrier air behind the front will allow for colder overnight lows\nSaturday Night, although typical for mid-October with readings\nmainly in the 30s.\n\nMonday through Wednesday: The next system approaches the region\nbut then slides south of the region into California. Ensembles\nhave come into good agreement with this idea with NBM now only\ncarrying a 5-10 percent chance of showers for early next week.\nHigh temperatures also show little day to day change with\nreadings in the upper 50s to mid 60s with is close to normal for\nthis time of year. JW",
